Description: Fix PipeWire deadlock in audio device enumeration
 Backport of upstream PR #27615 adapted to the Kodi 21 (Omega) PipeWire sink.
 .
 Two threads (the UI thread opening Settings > System > Audio and the ActiveAE
 thread) could enter CAESinkPipewire::EnumerateDevicesEx() concurrently. Each
 held the registry lock across loop.Wait(5s), which releases the PipeWire
 thread-loop lock while sleeping. The PipeWire callback thread then ran
 CPipewireRegistry::OnGlobalRemoved(), blocked acquiring the registry lock, and
 could no longer deliver the signal the sleeping thread was waiting for -> hang
 requiring a kill (upstream issue #27420). The registry lock had itself been
 added to fix a concurrent-map-mutation crash (issue #26212).
 .
 This switches enumeration to the upstream event-driven model: format
 enumeration is triggered from CPipewireNode::Info() when node params change and
 its results arrive asynchronously via Param(), which calls IAE::DeviceChange()
 to re-run enumeration. EnumerateDevicesEx() no longer waits, so it holds the
 thread-loop lock for the whole walk of the registry -> no concurrent mutation,
 making the registry lock redundant (its addition is reverted here).
 .
 Backported and adapted to Kodi 21 (Omega) by Vadim Mironov: this tree still
 has the CPipewireGlobal wrapper (registry.GetGlobals(), global->GetNode()) that
 master collapsed into CPipewireNode, and builds with C++17, so Info()'s
 std::span loop is rewritten as a plain index loop. No behavioural change from
 upstream.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/xbmc/xbmc/pull/27615
Bug: https://github.com/xbmc/xbmc/issues/27420
Bug: https://github.com/xbmc/xbmc/issues/26212
Applied-Upstream: 22.0, commit:b76776572de5cb457865326b9633a1e99c6b885a
Author: Markus Härer <markus.haerer@gmx.net>
